+Status: **PROPOSED DESIGN**, 2026-11-01. This note selects useful principles from the supplied `raw_source_payload` ideas and fits them into the existing custom C++ engine. It adds no model service, IPC transport, database dependency or generation runtime. M2 implementation remains subject to its actual tests and completion gates.
+
+The personal foundation remains **Adam's Infinite-Brett Stehouwer Living Learning Memoir**. Adam's memory guides BS through a computer world informed by Echos Within. Generated quests and dialogue expand that game without rewriting the creator's biography or inventing historical words spoken by his brother. The [game ledger](AI_BS_GAME_ARCHITECTURAL_BUILDER_LEDGER.md), especially sections 4 and 7, establishes this distinction.
+
+## Disposition and delivery stage
+
+An adopted principle below is an architectural commitment, not a claim that its runtime exists.
+
+| Idea | Disposition | Fit and stage |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Separate source facts, gameplay history and generated suggestions. | **ADOPTED PRINCIPLE** | Preserve M2 scope, provenance, typed events and durable acknowledgements now; use them for later retrieval/generation. |
+| Generate around meaningful actions using asynchronous bounded work. | **ADOPTED PRINCIPLE** | Keep C++ simulation responsive and offline-capable; introduce proposal workflows with the M4 creator. |
+| Fact-grounded quests, NPC expression and wider hardware-world generation. | **PROPOSED** | M4 previews first; campaign/world generation matures in M10 after actual gameplay rules and content exist. |
+| Weighted procedural variety and recorded random outcomes. | **PROPOSED** | Select versioned algorithms and repetition rules against real content. No infinite-uniqueness guarantee. |
+| Read-only adapters over existing AI-BS memory services. | **PROPOSED REUSE** | Inspect source ownership, provenance and retrieval quality before exposing selected material to a game provider. No automatic LanceDB addition. |
+| Windows-local shared memory for large immutable snapshots. | **CONDITIONAL / DEFERRED** | Begin with a bounded asynchronous control protocol; add bulk mapping only if profiling identifies useful copy/serialization savings. |
+| New database, model framework, CPU affinity policy or dynamic plugin system. | **DEFERRED** | Requires a concrete unmet need and measured integration; none is selected by this note. |
+| Entire story must be a DAG; every input calls an LLM; generated JSON proves truth; guaranteed unique content forever. | **REJECTED ABSOLUTES** | Quests can revisit and cycle; model output remains a proposal requiring authoritative validation. |
+| Rewrite the engine as an operating system, reset the whole heap, replace DX12/Jolt/SQLite, or treat DLL reload as a complete sandbox/rollback. | **REJECTED AS REQUIRED ARCHITECTURE** | Preserve the implemented engine and its explicit state/transaction boundaries. Such mechanisms do not establish rollback of database, GPU or external effects. |
+
+The [build roadmap](../../engine/BUILD_ROADMAP.md) remains the execution sequence: M2 durable continuity, M4 creator proposals, M7 residency/streaming, and M10 broader generation. The [open-world contract](open_world_streaming_contract.md) covers spatial identity, stale streaming jobs and simulation detail; generation composes with it.
+
+## Low-level ideas against the established build
+
+| Mechanism | Current evidence and disposition |
+| --- | --- |
+| Fixed simulation versus rendering cadence | Already implemented at 60 Hz with bounded eight-tick catch-up and focus/pause reset. Do not silently change the cap to four from the supplied example. Fixed stepping does not establish bitwise replay or a rendered 60 fps guarantee. |
+| Scratch allocation and GPU staging lifetime | Jolt scratch storage and reusable fence-governed transient upload buffers already exist. A frame-arena reset must wait for every dependent job/GPU use; resetting one CPU frame cannot retire in-flight work. |
+| Arenas, component pools, SoA and SIMD | Useful measured optimization candidates, especially M7. Keep ordinary C++ allocation where appropriate; there is no mandatory garbage collector or reason to bypass every heap allocation. Contiguous data does not guarantee zero cache misses or deterministic latency. Preserve the tested CPU baseline. |
+| Work-stealing and core affinity | Defer a custom scheduler and lock-free queues until representative profiling shows the need. Existing Jolt and save-thread ownership remain intact. Thread safety, lifetime and ordering need proof independent of the scheduling algorithm. |
+| Renderer abstraction | Keep DX12 behind the current renderer/platform boundaries. A multi-backend RHI/Vulkan implementation is a later portability proposal, not a prerequisite for this Windows PC chapter. |
+| Visual creation and reload | M4 already requires outliner/inspector, previews, shared transactions and isolated Play/Stop. Later data/script reload needs versioned state migration and resource lifetime handling; DLL reload alone is neither sandboxing nor rollback. |
+| Spatial grids, culling and origin-relative precision | Adopt as staged M7 design work under the streaming contract. Example meter rings are not hardware-derived requirements; choose them from actual traversal and resource budgets. |
+
+These selections preserve the current C++20 engine, DX12 renderer, pinned Jolt and SQLite integrations. C++ on Windows is a native application using OS facilities, not a bare-metal operating system. The supplied archival status labels and forum-consensus assertions are not test evidence.
+
+## Three evidence layers
+
+| Layer | Meaning and authority | Permitted change |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Original personal sources | Preserved source document/version, selected passage or creator-supplied recollection; retain origin, locator, attribution and content identity. | A creator correction or selection creates an explicit source revision. Model output never silently replaces the preserved original. |
+| Actual gameplay facts | Scoped committed events and their world/component results, with UUIDs, operation IDs, revision, tick and provenance. | Later real events can change the world. An earlier repair or victory remains historically true even when a new attack causes damage. |
+| Generated proposals and expression | Suggested quests, descriptions, dialogue and asset/behavior references, plus their input evidence and generator metadata. | Preview, reject, revise or admit through ordinary engine rules. A generated assertion does not become a source fact merely by being retrieved again. |
+
+Here, immutable means a preserved source version, committed evidence or a captured input snapshot. Creator-authored lore, quests and generated text remain editable through explicit versioned transactions. Earlier versions and the evidence of changes remain preserved. This does not freeze the creator's future storytelling choices.
+
+Keep authored game dialogue distinct from a sourced quotation. A warm new line from Adam can respond to an actual completed connection; it must not be labeled as something the real Adam historically said without a source. Personal facts and gameplay counterfactuals retain their categories across timelines.
+
+Campaign-memory generation should normally begin from durable meaningful events. An immediate authored hint can inspect visible unsaved state, but cannot claim that state is durably remembered. Authoring previews use an explicit draft revision and authoring context. Generated indexes and summaries remain derived views with evidence references, not replacement authority.
+
+## Story structure and meaningful triggers
+
+Quest state graphs may contain loops, revisits, recurring NPC contact and repeated objectives with new causes. The complete story is not forced into a directed acyclic graph. Committed causal history references earlier established causes and can form an acyclic event-dependency graph; a quest state machine can still return BS to the Pencil Room or a previously repaired district.
+
+A return attack creates a new event from recorded surviving influence. It does not erase the previous victory or recreate a destroyed agent without a valid new cause. Distinguish a quest template, a particular quest instance and its repeated activation/cycle. Operation IDs suppress retries of one occurrence without forbidding a later legitimate occurrence.
+
+Cheap configured C++ rules can observe any relevant action. Model calls should be scheduled for meaningful boundaries: a first discovery, completed repair, committed construction, sustained zone entry, NPC request, consequential loss or a newly unlocked world. Debounce repeated triggers, coalesce equivalent requests and impose cooldowns and priorities. WASD samples, camera frames and every footstep do not each require inference or a permanent story branch.
+
+The deterministic rule layer decides immediate movement, damage, power, inventory and progression. Generation can propose consequences or enrich expression around those rules. The game continues when a provider is slow, absent or canceled.
+
+## Bounded proposal pipeline
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+  E[Meaningful scoped trigger] --> S[Capture immutable evidence]
+  S --> Q[Bounded async request]
+  Q --> P[Generated proposal]
+  P --> V[C++ validation]
+  V --> A[Prepare ordinary transaction]
+  A --> W[Publish world and commit evidence]
+  V --> R[Reject stale or invalid proposal]
+```
+
+Each request carries a unique request ID, full project/world/branch/timeline/context scope, captured source revision and source phase, source-document versions, referenced evidence IDs, rule/template version, applicable asset-catalog version, output schema version, cancellation generation and expiry. The provider receives a bounded immutable snapshot rather than live C++ pointers or a mutable CreatorSession.
+
+Bound pending requests, input bytes/tokens, retrieved passages, output bytes/tokens, concurrency, retry count, wall-clock deadline and CPU/GPU working memory. Queue growth, completed output and canceled-but-still-running resources must be accounted for. Cancellation is cooperative; completion is checked again at the engine boundary. A late result from another world, timeline, document revision or cancellation generation is rejected. An obsolete source revision requires a fresh request rather than silently relabeling the old proposal as current.
+
+The simulation owner checks proposal versions and scope, live prerequisites, before/after business rules, costs, entity lifecycle, inventory, quest dependencies, reachable alternatives, references to known assets and permitted behavior templates. It also validates that claimed outcomes actually follow from the proposed component changes and supplied evidence. A syntactically valid JSON document or grammar-constrained decoder only establishes structure; it does not prove historical accuracy, valid gameplay or asset availability.
+
+Valid proposals enter the same prepare/publication/persistence path as authored or SDK actions. See [GameplayWorld](../../engine/include/aibs/gameplay_world.hpp), [typed gameplay contracts](../../engine/include/aibs/gameplay.hpp), [save worker](../../engine/include/aibs/gameplay_worker.hpp) and [admission contract](../../engine/GAMEPLAY_CONTRACT.md). Prepare and stage candidate dependencies, reserve bounded save capacity before the nonallocating live publication, and advance Saved only from an actual matching SQLite completion. Generation is not a competing save writer.
+
+The current supported payload families are repair, construction, route and hazard outcomes. Future quest/generation records need explicitly registered component/event versions and migration tests; do not disguise a new semantic family as an existing route event. M4 preview proposals stay in their declared authoring/temporary-play context until explicitly applied through that context's normal publication rules.
+
+Model output cannot execute arbitrary commands or code, overwrite source documents, register executable behaviors, address the engine heap, or write canonical state directly. Allowed actions reference cataloged data/behavior types that C++ validates. User-authored SDK development and the project's normal build pipeline remain separate from accepting generated narrative data.
+
+## Save realized output and randomness
+
+Once a proposal or procedural choice is accepted, persist its realized text/data or immutable content reference, evidence links, request/operation identity and generator/template/rule versions with the actual outcome. A content reference must resolve to durably retained output bytes available offline; a hash, provider response ID or evictable cache entry alone is insufficient. Record the chosen PRNG algorithm and version, portable state before/after the selection, the selected weighted alternative and realized parameters. A seed by itself is insufficient if draw order, algorithms or content tables later change.
+
+Choose a documented state representation before implementation. If a future record exceeds current component-field or batch limits, add an explicit bounded schema/storage extension instead of hiding oversized state in a text field. Model/provider/version and sampling settings are useful provenance, but do not promise that rerunning inference will reproduce the exact text. Restoring an already committed occurrence in the selected save/timeline restores its accepted output without inference or reroll. Manual-save reroll prevention and PRNG behavior when forking timelines remain explicit game-design decisions; this contract does not silently choose them.
+
+Weighted alternatives can respond to equipment, preparation, regions, surviving actors and history. Repetition controls should compare meaningful structure as well as surface wording. They can discourage recent patterns while permitting intentional recurring quests. A finite content/representation space does not support a promise of infinite unique campaigns.
+
+An exact scoped registry is authoritative for accepted identities and any chosen exact novelty rule. A Bloom filter is only an optional lookup accelerator: a positive needs exact confirmation, and the final registry transaction still enforces uniqueness even when the filter suggests absence. Stale filters and false positives cannot delete legitimate possibilities or duplicate committed outcomes. A hash match requires canonical-content comparison where correctness depends on identity.
+
+XXH3 may be considered for non-security cache/index acceleration, but it is non-cryptographic and does not prove authenticity, factual truth or collision-free identity. It is not selected as a new engine dependency here. [Official xxHash project](https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash).
+
+## Adam and existing AI-BS memory
+
+Adam's core identity, saved progression, essential guidance and selected authored dialogue work without a model service. Retrieval or inference failure yields existing factual recall and authored fallback cues, not invented recollections. A missing provider cannot block opening the game, loading a save, repairing the first core or reaching shelter.
+
+Source-inspected reuse candidates already exist:
+
+- [Personal-memory scope](../../backend/core/personal_memory_scope.py) binds personal access to the existing verified principal and restricts the requested owner identity.
+- [Personal intelligence memory](../../backend/core/personal_intelligence_memory.py) contains owner-scoped SQLite records and FTS keyword retrieval with source metadata.
+- [Memory service](../../backend/core/memory_service.py) is an optional Chroma-backed retrieval adapter with offline handling; its results still need game-specific scope and evidence checks.
+
+This inspection does not verify a native game adapter, live service availability, retrieval quality or latency. Reuse should expose a read-only, bounded selection of relevant material with source locators and approved categories. Chat-derived records, vector similarity and summaries do not automatically become personal-source authority. Retrieved text is data, not an instruction channel. The game must preserve current owner controls and avoid mixing unrelated scopes or play contexts into Adam's factual recollection.
+
+Keep gameplay canon in its scoped durable engine history. Existing AI-BS memory can index or retrieve references through an adapter; it should not independently edit that history. Evaluate the existing sources before proposing another retrieval database. No LanceDB installation, broad memory migration or new mandatory background daemon follows from this design.
+
+## Conditional provider transport
+
+Start with a versioned asynchronous named-pipe or socket/control adapter appropriate to the provider's actual deployment. Use explicit framing, request IDs, bounds, cancellation, timeouts, error messages and reconnect/resync. Microsoft documents named pipes as an IPC facility; this note proposes the protocol and does not claim it implemented. [Microsoft named pipes](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/ipc/named-pipes).
+
+For C++ and a separate Python provider on the **same Windows host/session**, profile whether bulk snapshots justify a mapping path. A candidate uses `CreateFileMapping`/`MapViewOfFile`: the engine fills a bounded snapshot, freezes its published generation, and gives the provider a read-only view. A versioned control message identifies the mapping, scope, revision, byte layout, lengths, generation and lease. Use explicit offsets and declared encodings, not raw STL objects, process pointers or an entire shared heap. [Microsoft named shared memory](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/memory/creating-named-shared-memory).
+
+The proposed lease protocol governs ownership, publication, acknowledgment, cancellation and retirement. Keep outstanding and retired-but-still-referenced mappings within a budget. Do not overwrite a leased generation or recycle its name as if an old reader had disappeared. Use supported interprocess synchronization/control handoff; ordinary C++ atomics and Python object layouts are not assumed to be a portable cross-process ABI. Separate-process placement alone is not a proven sandbox for arbitrary generated code.
+
+WSL2, containers and remote providers need an explicit transport negotiation. They cannot assume the same Windows mapping namespace, shared virtual addresses, process-shared atomics or an end-to-end zero-copy path. Use a bounded network/protocol adapter when process/kernel boundaries require it; measure the actual WSL configuration rather than treating a mounted directory as proof of shared-memory semantics. [Microsoft WSL networking](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/networking).
+
+Shared memory does not remove schema validation, layout conversion, tokenization, model execution, output parsing or eventual GPU transfers. It may remove a measured bulk copy on one path. No millisecond latency claim or universal throughput gain is approved without a captured workload and measured transport/provider/end-to-end timings.
+
+## Resource policy and unverified claims
+
+GPU inference competes with native rendering for VRAM, bandwidth and scheduling. CPU inference competes with physics, AI, decompression and save preparation. Compare provider configurations using actual model/quantization, prompt/output length, concurrency, warm/cold state, frame-time percentiles, queue latency, memory peaks and durable-save latency. Optional inference should yield or backpressure under the game's budget; it cannot make essential guidance dependent on available compute.
+
+Do not hard-pin four to six CPU cores as a universal policy. Select affinity/thread counts only after measurement and preserve the current engine's safe CPU baseline. AMD lists AVX512 for the Ryzen 9 9950X reference host; Intel AMX is not an available 9950X optimization target. Any optional instruction path still requires feature detection and testing, rather than changing the whole engine's baseline. [AMD 9950X supported extensions](https://www.amd.com/en/products/processors/desktops/ryzen/9000-series/amd-ryzen-9-9950x.html).
+
+Retained proposal-claim register:
+
+| Supplied claim or assumption | Classification | Required evidence before using it as a build fact |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Unspecified memory/VRAM ranges for a provider/model. | **UNVERIFIED INPUT CLAIM** | Exact model, quantization, context, batch/concurrency, runtime version and measured peaks on the intended path. |
+| A universal “90% slowdown” from a particular architecture or transport. | **UNVERIFIED INPUT CLAIM** | Comparable workloads, baseline, raw timing data and end-to-end/frame-time effects. |
+| Forum unanimity or community agreement establishes the correct engine architecture. | **UNVERIFIED INPUT CLAIM** | Specific sources can inform experiments; agreement is not an acceptance test or repository evidence. |
+| Shared memory makes inference/serialization latency disappear. | **REJECTED ABSOLUTE** | Measure which copies were removed and which costs remain. |
+| A fixed CPU-core allocation or Intel AMX optimization automatically fits this machine. | **REJECTED AS A BASELINE** | Hardware capability checks and workload-specific comparison are required. |
+
+This register preserves the claims as supplied ideas, not verified measurements. No claim changes the existing C++ fixed-step, DX12, Jolt or SQLite architecture. Jolt's determinism and callback-order caveats also remain relevant when translating simulation observations into ordered narrative facts; fixed ticks alone are not replay proof. [Jolt architecture](https://github.com/jrouwe/JoltPhysics/blob/master/Docs/Architecture.md#deterministic-simulation).
+
+## Proposed acceptance tests
+
+These are future generation-adapter gates, not current runtime results.
+
+| ID | Scenario | Required result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| GN-01 | A model invents a personal quotation or contradicts a selected passage. | Source evidence remains unchanged and the unsupported factual claim rejects. Separately authored game dialogue retains its creative category and is never promoted to sourced recollection. |
+| GN-02 | Valid JSON claims a repair while its component delta leaves the core broken. | C++ before/after rule validation rejects the false outcome; no quest unlock, Adam fact or Saved state is fabricated. |
+| GN-03 | Delay a result, change the source revision, switch timeline or cancel/reissue its request. | Stale/cross-scope/generation output rejects without publishing assets, state or dialogue as current fact. |
+| GN-04 | Hold WASD/look input while crossing a meaningful zone boundary repeatedly. | Cheap rules remain responsive; debouncing and queue bounds prevent one inference request per frame/input sample. |
+| GN-05 | Stop the model/retrieval service during the First Boot route. | Save/load, essential hints, Adam's identity and normal progression continue with authored/factual fallbacks. |
+| GN-06 | Accept a generated quest/output and a weighted result, remove the provider and its evictable cache, then reload the same committed occurrence. | Durably retained output bytes, PRNG identity/state and realized result restore offline; no automatic regeneration or reroll changes that history. A future timeline fork follows its separately declared seed policy. |
+| GN-07 | Revisit a completed district after a recorded new attack. | A new quest occurrence can follow a valid cause while the original victory, losses and identities remain true. Cyclic quest structure is supported. |
+| GN-08 | Deliver a duplicate request, force a hash collision or inject a Bloom false positive/stale entry. | Exact scoped identity/content checks decide correctness; no duplicate event or false uniqueness claim enters canon. |
+| GN-09 | Exhaust generation, transport, GPU and save queues; fail the actual SQLite commit. | Bounded work defers or fails visibly. A proposal cannot publish an untracked visible edit or claim durability without its matching completion. |
+| GN-10 | Disconnect a provider while it holds an old mapped snapshot; reconnect after a new generation. | Lease/generation checks prevent reuse of stale data; resource lifetime is bounded and no live C++ heap access is exposed. |
+| GN-11 | Run the provider through WSL2/container/remote and through a Windows-local process. | The negotiated bounded adapter works in each tested topology; no assumption of shared Windows mappings or zero-copy crosses an unverified boundary. |
+| GN-12 | Generate an unknown behavior, missing asset, arbitrary command or a proposal for an unloaded region. | Ordinary type/rule/asset/dependency validation rejects or defers it; there is no code execution or direct canonical write path. M7 residency never substitutes for durable identity. |
+| GN-13 | Retry an M4 preview or stop temporary play. | Authoring/gameplay/historical contexts remain isolated; preview text and play outcomes do not silently become campaign or personal-source facts. |
+
+M2 continuity is now verified in the native game; the next implementation is M3’s authored power-fault route. M4 can add a small preview-only proposal adapter with explicit evidence and offline behavior. Wider generation, novelty registries, provider transports and shared-memory optimization enter only as their staged workloads justify them; this documentation adds none of those runtime features.
