+# Open-world streaming and simulation residency — proposed M7 contract
+
+Status: **PROPOSED**, 2026-11-01. This is a design note for M7, with compatibility requirements for M2. It does not implement streaming, floating origin, simulation tiers or a new component-storage backend. The motherboard world and movement course currently use small fixed scenes. M2 remains governed by its implementation evidence and acceptance gates; this note does not mark it complete.
+
+The scope follows [BUILD_ROADMAP](../../engine/BUILD_ROADMAP.md), especially M2, M7, M8 and M9, and the ongoing [game ledger](AI_BS_GAME_ARCHITECTURAL_BUILDER_LEDGER.md), sections 7–11. The aim is a larger computer world where BS's construction, claimed architecture, vehicles, agents and Adam's recollections remain coherent while the engine controls its working set. Jenison, the shelter and Pencil Room retain their authored meaning and persistent identities when their geometry leaves memory. The creator can still revise authored lore and quests through explicit versioned transactions; streaming does not rewrite them or prevent their deliberate editing.
+
+## Proposed choices and rationale
+
+| Concern | Proposed direction | Reason and unresolved choice |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Spatial partition | Addressable three-dimensional cells, with overlapping residency bands around relevant activity. | The motherboard includes elevated components, conduits, interiors and towers. A flat tile map would omit useful vertical separation. Cell dimensions and whether all regions use one partition scale remain open. |
+| Loading policy | Bounded prefetch, preparation and activation; hysteresis when retiring cells. | Movement speed, view changes and coupled machinery require lookahead. Pure distance thresholds would repeatedly load and unload the same boundary. |
+| Coordinates | High-precision authoritative locations; nearby float coordinates for current render/physics adapters. | Large travel should preserve small construction details. Signed cell indices plus a double-precision local offset and world-space doubles are candidates; the durable representation, error budget and rebase threshold need measured selection. |
+| Simulation | Independent active, coarse and dormant policies, separate from visual LOD. | A distant contested sector can need strategic updates while its detailed geometry is absent. A visible landmark can need rendering without full local physics. |
+| Hot component data | Evaluate packed chunks for entities sharing component layouts behind AI-BS interfaces. | Grouping hot data may reduce scattered access. Profiling must choose the actual storage scheme; this does not select EnTT, promise an archetype implementation or change saved component meaning. |
+| Existing dependencies | Preserve the Jolt physics adapter and SQLite durability boundary. | Residency belongs above these adapters. A larger world does not justify replacing working dependencies or changing their build profiles silently. |
+
+No numeric cell size, active radius, upload allowance or performance improvement is approved by this proposal. Establish them against captured workloads, including the creator's current PC and later reference hardware. The roadmap's 1080p/60 fps reference remains a target requiring measurements.
+
+## M2 invariants to preserve now
+
+The [gameplay admission contract](../../engine/GAMEPLAY_CONTRACT.md), [typed gameplay API](../../engine/include/aibs/gameplay.hpp), [SQLite store](../../engine/include/aibs/gameplay_store.hpp) and [bounded save worker](../../engine/include/aibs/gameplay_worker.hpp) define the durability boundary. Streaming must compose with that boundary.
+
+1. Every persistent entity, construction assembly, agent, item and story reference retains its UUID. Project, world, branch, timeline and context remain explicit scope. Cell addresses, local array indices, Jolt body IDs, GPU offsets and residency generations are runtime/indexing data, never substitute saved identities.
+2. A cell holds spatial indexes into canonical entities. Repartitioning or moving an entity between cells preserves its UUID, ownership, component versions, operation receipts and history. A named region or personal-memory anchor retains its own identity even if its bounds span multiple cells.
+3. Unloading, LOD changes and origin shifts are representation operations. They do not delete canonical components, consume inventory, award discoveries, claim territory or create story/Adam outcomes. An explicit gameplay demolition is a separate validated operation with its actual consequences.
+4. Visible world publication and durable SQLite completion remain distinct. Eviction cannot turn an unsaved visible edit into a saved one. Keep the immutable pending changes and an intelligible recovery state under existing queue bounds; apply backpressure before losing work. Only a successful matching storage completion advances the durable head.
+5. Source memories, authored dialogue and actual gameplay outcomes remain distinguishable. Adam may explain a loading boundary using authored presentation, but cannot remember that BS crossed a route, won a fight or restored a machine merely because its cell was activated.
+6. New spatial records require an explicit component type/version and migration path. Do not reinterpret existing numeric `x/y/z` fields or format-1 checkpoint evidence as a different coordinate encoding. Preserve imported original evidence and old operation identities.
+7. Disk-resident entity metadata and dependency endpoints remain resolvable without instantiating their graphics or physics. An unloaded target is neither automatically invalid nor automatically destroyed; rule validation must consult canonical state and the declared dependency policy.
+
+These requirements reserve compatibility. They do not require adding M7 cell fields to current M2 saves before the representation is selected.
+
+## Location, origin and asynchronous work
+
+The authoritative spatial record must identify its coordinate space and retain enough precision for the chosen world extent and smallest supported edits. Convert from high-precision world coordinates relative to a high-precision local origin before producing adapter floats. Reject invalid ranges or unsupported conversions before activation. The exact stored units and representation remain open.
+
+An origin change occurs at a simulation publication boundary. Coordinate conversion must cover the player, bodies, constraints, construction transforms, navigation queries, audio positions, particles, selection rays and interpolation history consistently. Relative geometry and velocities retain their meaning. Render motion history must be transformed correctly or explicitly invalidated. Reconstruct disposable adapter state from canonical data where that is safer than patching it. A rebase alone creates no gameplay transaction or Adam event.
+
+Every asynchronous request carries scope, cell address, a residency generation, layout/content version, and the source/dependency revisions used to build its result. Local-space results also carry an origin epoch. Per-cell content revisions can avoid invalidating unrelated work, but their validation must still establish consistency with the authoritative source snapshot. Cancellation is advisory: the simulation owner checks tokens again before publication. A late decompression, mesh, navigation or upload job cannot resurrect removed geometry, publish an older edit, or install coordinates for a previous origin.
+
+CPU jobs operate on bounded immutable input. They return prepared results to the owner; they do not mutate a live CreatorSession or write independent save heads. Origin-independent source data can be reused after a rebase; an origin-dependent result must be rebuilt or explicitly transformed and revalidated.
+
+## Residency bands and activation safety
+
+Proposed cell lifecycle:
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+  A[Absent from runtime] --> R[Requested]
+  R --> D[Decoded source]
+  D --> P[Prepared dependencies]
+  P --> V[Active adapters]
+  V --> T[Retiring]
+  T --> A
+  T --> V
+```
+
+Canonical saved state exists independently throughout this lifecycle. Failures leave the last valid publication in place and provide a bounded retry or an explicit unavailable state. Preparation and activation are separate so a decoded mesh cannot accidentally imply ready collision.
+
+The inner band retains gameplay-critical collision and dependencies. A preparation band stages upcoming collision/navigation and render resources. An outer band prefetches source data. Load and unload thresholds differ, with a minimum residency interval to limit churn. Priorities consider three-dimensional distance, velocity, stopping distance, predicted travel, camera direction, recent visibility and memory pressure. The fast skimmer or an abrupt teleport requires a different readiness envelope from walking BS.
+
+Relevant anchors include the player, coupled vehicle/structure bounds, approaching agents, active objectives and critical routes. Anchors and dependency closures also have hard budgets and priorities; thousands of quest markers cannot pin the whole world indefinitely. Optional visual detail yields before required safety data.
+
+If the next movement region lacks valid collision, hold travel at a known safe boundary, slow or stop the vehicle under a declared rule, or defer a teleport until readiness succeeds. Preserve a safe return point and show the loading/failure state. Rendering distant terrain does not authorize entry through missing collision. Do not treat a temporarily unavailable route as permanently destroyed or record arrival before the crossing happens.
+
+## Construction and dependencies across cells
+
+A bridge, cable, coolant loop, power network or agent route may cross many cells. Store explicit UUID references and spatial/dependency footprints. Choose a canonical owner for each assembly; other cells maintain indexes/references to that owner. Loading two neighboring cells must not duplicate the assembly, its inventory or its damage history.
+
+Before publishing a build, dismantle or upgrade, resolve its complete affected set: geometry, collision, support, navigation, power/cooling connections, ownership and quest/NPC references. Stage replacement adapters and invalidate dependent jobs with content-generation tokens. Publish the validated gameplay result through the shared operation/admission path, with matching semantic outcomes and eventual atomic persistence. If a required region cannot be checked or staged within the budget, reject or defer with a reason instead of publishing half a valid bridge.
+
+Coupled physics bodies and constraints require an activation closure. Keep the necessary connected group active, transfer it through a validated handoff, or suspend it coherently under an explicit rule. Retiring one cell cannot detach half a moving vehicle or erase the support beneath a machine. Navigation routes crossing retired or rebuilt tiles must revalidate links and offer reachable alternatives; power dependencies remain meaningful while their render cells are absent.
+
+Large terrain/volume edits must respect existing batch and worker limits. A future chunked draft plus a bounded publication manifest is a candidate; splitting one atomic operation into unrelated successful saves is not an approved shortcut. Any new manifest, asset-reference or transaction format needs its own version, migration and failure proof.
+
+## Simulation detail and fact continuity
+
+| Policy | Proposed work | Required continuity |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Active | Detailed nearby collision, movement, interactions and relevant agent decisions at their declared update rates. | Canonical entities retain identity while adapter objects appear or disappear. Necessary collision stays ready before travel. |
+| Coarse | Budgeted strategic rules for production, supply, contested regions, orders and travel during active play, when those systems are implemented in M9. | Validated outcomes use the same scoped operation/event/persistence path. Loading the region realizes recorded state rather than inventing what happened. |
+| Dormant | Retain facts and dependency metadata while no scheduled simulation requires updates. | Dormancy does not imply death, reset, completion or elapsed strategic progress. Relevant pending work can promote the region under budget. Ongoing warfare, logistics and production require coarse scheduling during active play even while their geometry is absent; cell unloading cannot silently freeze them. |
+
+The scheduler tracks a handoff simulation tick so two representations cannot advance the same entity twice. Durable simulation progress and rule/random-state provenance accompany actual gameplay outcomes; a pure policy transition is transient scheduler bookkeeping and adds no story event. Visual mesh/texture LOD is independently selected. Lower physics or AI detail must declare which interactions it approximates and which safety/quest outcomes require promotion to active simulation.
+
+The selected game rule remains: warfare and campaign simulation advance only during active play and pause with play. Pause/close does not trigger wall-clock catch-up, offscreen victories or losses on reopen. Already accepted disk writes, IO and GPU completion may finish while simulation is paused; they do not advance its clock. The phone companion follows the authoritative PC session.
+
+Offscreen outcomes need actual rule evaluation, bounded scheduling and durable evidence. A surviving enemy, blocked supply route or destroyed agent cannot be silently replaced when detailed NPCs respawn. If save backpressure prevents admitting a consequential outcome, defer that outcome coherently rather than narrating an unrecorded result. Existing personal facts and Adam's memory remain authoritative inputs to later generated stories.
+
+## Budgets, data layout and resource lifetime
+
+| Budget | Required accounting and exhaustion behavior |
+| --- | --- |
+| CPU source/decompression/cooking | Cap outstanding requests, encoded/expanded bytes, job concurrency and publication work. Validate sizes before expanding untrusted content. Cancel stale work and reclaim it after consumers finish. |
+| Simulation | Measure active bodies, contacts/constraints, AI work, dependency evaluation and coarse updates. Spread optional work without silently skipping required gameplay outcomes. |
+| CPU/GPU rendering | Bound per-frame staging bytes, uploads, descriptors and activation work. Keep a ready lower detail representation when safe; required collision has its own readiness gate. |
+| VRAM and fences | Account for resident, uploading and retiring resources together. Resources remain alive until their copy/graphics consumers and relevant frame fences finish. Unloading a cell cannot immediately recycle a live GPU address or descriptor. |
+| Saves and completions | Include queued, inflight and undrained completion storage in queue credits. Retain failed work under the established retry/close contract. Cell eviction cannot bypass backpressure or create a competing SQLite writer. |
+| Disk | Track source assets, cooked caches, database/WAL growth and local free space. Cleanup applies only to verified disposable caches under the roadmap's retention policy; protect saves, original memories/art and marked milestones. |
+
+Packed component chunks are a proposed runtime optimization. Persist registered component values and UUID relationships rather than raw chunk memory or pointer addresses. Structural edits move entities only at controlled publication points; generation-checked runtime handles and immutable job views must detect stale storage. Decide whether packed archetypes, sparse sets or groups fit real workloads before replacing the current small-world storage. Profile data movement as well as arithmetic.
+
+## Technical guidance and source boundaries
+
+Jolt distinguishes broadphase candidate filtering from detailed collision and CCD. Fast small bodies need a tested motion-quality/sweep policy; broadphase overlap alone cannot prevent tunneling. Its large-world guidance also describes precision limitations and double-position/local-offset approaches. Deterministic simulation requires controlled modification order and compatible binaries/settings, with ordering caveats for queries and callbacks. These support the precision, collision and scheduling constraints above; AI-BS's cell policies remain proposed engine decisions. [Jolt architecture](https://github.com/jrouwe/JoltPhysics/blob/master/Docs/Architecture.md#broad-phase), [CCD](https://github.com/jrouwe/JoltPhysics/blob/master/Docs/Architecture.md#continuous-collision-detection), [big worlds](https://github.com/jrouwe/JoltPhysics/blob/master/Docs/Architecture.md#big-worlds), [determinism](https://github.com/jrouwe/JoltPhysics/blob/master/Docs/Architecture.md#deterministic-simulation).
+
+Fixed 60 Hz is a scheduling choice, not evidence of bitwise deterministic gameplay or physics. Do not infer replay correctness from tick rate alone. SIMD should be evaluated through measured hot-loop throughput and suitable data layout; unsupported claims such as dozens of complete matrix transforms in one CPU cycle do not establish a budget.
+
+The linked upstream `master` document is guidance and can evolve. The engine's existing Jolt 5.6.0 pin/profile remains authoritative for current API/build behavior; see the [dependency lock](../../engine/cmake/JoltDependency.lock.json) and [current physics slice](../../engine/PHYSICS_SLICE.md). This proposal changes neither Jolt precision flags nor SQLite's existing durability/ownership contract.
+
+## Acceptance scenarios before M7 can be marked verified
+
+All scenarios below are **PROPOSED acceptance tests**, not current results. Capture actual frame/resource budgets, scoped revisions, queue status and persistent identities alongside the behavioral result.
+
+| ID | Scenario | Required observable result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| OW-01 | Build a relay/bridge across a cell edge; depart, unload, return and restart. | One canonical assembly, original UUIDs, inventory costs, support/power/nav references and actual Adam outcomes survive. No duplicate build or residency event enters story history. |
+| OW-02 | Unload while an edit is visible but not durable; force a write failure or terminate the process. | Eviction retains pending immutable work while the process runs, and UI never claims Saved without acknowledgment. After termination, restart exposes the atomic durable head and never presents uncommitted edits as saved. |
+| OW-03 | Rebase while standing on a slope, crossing a bridge or occupying a vehicle. | Relative pose, contacts, velocities, constraints and selection remain coherent within measured error; no inventory, discovery, territory or Adam event changes because of the rebase. |
+| OW-04 | Remove/rebuild a cell while its old mesh/nav/upload job is delayed; complete that old job later. | Scope, generation, dependency revision and origin checks reject the stale publication. Removed objects never reappear. |
+| OW-05 | Sprint/drive through a boundary, reverse direction rapidly and rotate the view. | Hysteresis limits churn. Required cells become ready within the tested envelope or travel stops safely before missing collision; memory/upload budgets stay bounded. |
+| OW-06 | Request a teleport or objective arrival into a failed/missing collision cell. | Activation fails or defers with a safe return option. BS does not fall through rendered ground, and no arrival/quest completion is invented. |
+| OW-07 | Dismantle a cross-cell power link, support or navigation route while a consumer is unloaded. | Dependency state updates once, stale paths invalidate, canonical consumers remain resolvable, and the declared alternate progression/recovery path works after reload. |
+| OW-08 | Move a coupled assembly across a cell boundary under activation pressure. | Constraints and ownership remain intact; a coherent group is retained, handed off or suspended. A cell cannot retire half the assembly. |
+| OW-09 | Let a distant contested sector advance under coarse rules, then approach it. | Active representations match recorded strategic state. No duplicated agents, invented victories or resurrected destroyed assets appear. |
+| OW-10 | Pause, minimize under the selected pause policy, close and reopen during strategic activity. | Simulation ticks/outcomes stop under the declared rule. Completing previously accepted IO creates no elapsed-war catch-up; the companion cannot advance a closed campaign. |
+| OW-11 | Exhaust decode memory, upload capacity, VRAM, save credits and disk space separately. | Bounded deferral or explicit failure preserves the last valid world. Retiring GPU resources remain alive until fences finish; queue counters include undrained completions. |
+| OW-12 | Change component layout or cell partition; load an older save and retry an old operation. | Explicit migrations preserve scope, UUIDs, receipts and sourced memory. Runtime storage moves never become saved pointer identities or duplicate semantic events. |
+| OW-13 | Visit equal cell coordinates in another world/timeline; deliver a delayed job from the original session. | Complete scope validation rejects cross-session publication; progress and Adam's records remain in their intended world/timeline. |
+
+Implementation order: M2's atomic durable gameplay, recovery and original-save import are verified in the native game; preserve those compatibility invariants as M3–M6 add real rules and content. Implement M7 streaming incrementally with a small boundary test world, then cross-cell construction/dependencies, origin handling and measured working-set budgets. Vehicle-scale readiness follows M8, and strategic simulation continuity is integrated with M9. This note records a design and adds no M7 runtime.
