+# Sheltered hub and Pencil Room story design
+
+Status: PROPOSED, planning only. Source basis: selected passages of Echos Within, especially Adam My Brother Always, the pencil passage, remembered shared play and laughter, and the description of home as wherever Mom and Adam lived. All rooms, NPCs, quests, consequences and new dialogue below are fictional adaptation proposals. They do not assert additional biographical events or words spoken by Adam.
+
+## Emotional foundation
+
+Confirmed creator correction: Jenison is the unwanted starting base, an antagonistic place full of enemies who never believed in BS. Jenison itself is not the safe home represented by Adam's memory. The creator selects the negative figures described in Echos Within as the source cast for antagonists. Map each portrayal to the manuscript's account, preserving its names/aliases and distinguishing recalled events from newly invented game behavior.
+
+The sheltered hub is a place BS creates or discovers within that larger journey. Its final location is unresolved: a protected foothold inside Jenison, a refuge beyond its boundary, or a later player-built home. It embodies the creator's remembered feeling of safety with Adam and family. Adam's support remains steady across branches, failed repairs and endings. Player choices change the built world and relationships with fictional inhabitants; they do not change the facts of the creator's life or make support something to earn.
+
+BS begins in unwanted Jenison. A proposed first shelter is a small secured workshop or hidden room, distinct from the hostile district around it; another route can lead to shelter outside Jenison. Warm light, a workbench and places to sit are things BS establishes as safety develops. Furnishing, room shape, paths and machine layout are editable. The Pencil Room's full development follows that established foothold rather than implying Jenison was already home.
+
+## Walkable layout
+
+Arrival vestibule -> shared living space -> pencil worktable -> Pencil Room.
+The living space also connects to the machine bay, a sheltered garden/overlook and the Core Sinks service door. A maintenance loop can reconnect the machine bay to the vestibule. Alternative stairs, lifts, tunnels and bridges can change that topology.
+
+| Place | Detail and source connection | Initial interaction | Consequence possibilities |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Arrival vestibule | A threshold into the feeling of home described in the manuscript; no claim to reconstruct a real house | Enter, pause, inspect power, choose which inner door to open | Entry route establishes available sightlines, clues and first NPC contact |
+| Shared living space | Remembered shared gaming and laughter; proposed paired seats and an initially dormant game display | Sit, switch on a game, arrange furniture, speak with visitors | Opens play-oriented tasks, visitor conversations and an optional return motif |
+| Pencil worktable | The manuscript's pencil as a tool for preserving memories | Pick up pencil, write, sketch a connection, organize a chosen page | Introduces preservation and creation through different first actions |
+| Pencil Room | A personal writing/archive room whose layout grows from selected memories | Read, arrange, write captions, create displays, choose what is shared | Produces different archive paths, contextual recall and visitor responses |
+| Machine bay | A fictional expression of building things to share and enjoy | Restore power, assemble a machine, reroute capacity | Can power the archive, game display, lift or a visitor's requested device |
+| Sheltered garden/overlook | A proposed place for comfortable silence and looking toward what comes next | Rest, plant/build, view routes, invite a visitor | Opens environmental discoveries and different departure paths |
+| Service threshold | Connects the safe home base to Core Sinks | Open door, inspect, build an alternate exit, ask for guidance | Changes discovery order, supply access and available encounter approaches |
+
+The sheltered rooms remain a dependable place of return. Hazardous construction can be previewed or isolated in a test bay; deliberate game rules decide how experiments affect surrounding machinery. Ordinary mistakes never secretly revoke the hub's emotional safety.
+
+## Pencil Room in detail
+
+The first view is a modest desk beneath a warm task light, with a pencil, blank paper and one selected piece of writing. Beyond it are empty shelves and movable display surfaces. The room can become intimate, spacious, workshop-like or gallery-like through player construction.
+
+Proposed stations:
+
+- Writing desk: preserve a selected memory in words, sketch a structure or make a private note.
+- Memory shelves: arrange chosen writings and objects by relationship, place, theme or the player's own system.
+- Shared-play corner: an optional game setup that gives joy and ordinary companionship space in the story.
+- Creation wall: turn a sketch into a proposed structure, inspect the design and build it through the existing creation tools.
+- Route journal: retain actual journeys, shortcuts and unfinished intentions, distinct from biographical memories.
+- Door to the wider archive: opens through several routes, including restoring machinery, finding a writing route or helping a visitor recover a catalog reference.
+
+The pencil has three proposed modes: Record (write/preserve), Trace (understand a connection), and Sketch (propose a build). A chosen memory can inspire a structure without becoming a spendable currency. Source writings remain preserved while displays and fictional interpretations can be rearranged.
+
+Potential personal objects are proposals for selection, not automatic canon: a pencil; a shared-game setup; a red toy-truck motif drawn from a remembered happy moment; a page about Adam believing in his brother; a threshold/home motif. Specific photographs, handwriting, recordings, dates and private passages require deliberate content selection rather than invented substitutes presented as authentic.
+
+## Actions and branching outcomes
+
+The creator can attach configurable rules to every relevant action category: movement/arrival, leaving, looking, inspecting, opening/closing, sitting, speaking, building, modifying, deleting, repairing, connecting, writing and taking a route. Movement rules use zones, crossings, dwell or sequences; individual footsteps need not create permanent branches.
+
+Action -> validated event -> conditions -> state changes -> world/NPC/quest consequences -> Adam's scoped recall.
+
+Every action can be a rule input. Its current configuration may produce an immediate change, a delayed condition, contextual dialogue, a recorded fact or no visible consequence. This supports broad authorship without promising infinitely many individually scripted endings.
+
+Each rule defines stable ID/version, event, subjects/zone, conditions, priority, effects, cooldown, once/repeat policy, relevant history, reversibility and player feedback. Rules can be edited in visual tools, code or AI-assisted authoring through the same project model. Changing a rule during Play creates a declared sandbox revision; publishing it to an existing save needs compatibility handling.
+
+| Action | Example conditions | Proposed consequence | Later consequence |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Open the service door early | Core Sinks still dark | Meet the fictional Courier and find a maintenance clue | Obtain a supply route before restoring the archive |
+| Inspect the desk first | Pencil not yet used | Discover the writing path | Archives can become the first major destination |
+| Build a generator | Components available and valid placement | Restore one selected consumer circuit | Power priorities influence which visitor arrives and which task becomes available |
+| Build a bridge | Reachable endpoints and collision validation | Create an actual alternate route | Courier changes path; Adam recalls this player-built shortcut |
+| Close a bypass door | Its pressure/electrical rule is configured | Stabilize one branch while rerouting another | A different access puzzle or ventilation route becomes useful |
+| Move a shelf | It is movable and the gap remains navigable | Reveal an optional maintenance panel | Unlock a hidden workspace configuration |
+| Talk before repairing | NPC knows the fault's location | Learn a safer repair approach | NPC acknowledges being consulted; new cooperation task |
+| Repair before talking | Fault already repaired | NPC recognizes the actual completed work | Follow-up task replaces the obsolete tutorial |
+| Leave a task unfinished | No irreversible deadline declared | Record an unfinished intention | NPC offers current alternatives on return, rather than repeating stale instructions |
+| Dismantle a machine | World policy permits it | Reclaim parts and remove its supplied capability | Dependent quests pause or reroute with explained recovery options |
+
+Opening a door is not automatically a moral decision. Meaning follows the configured situation and visible consequences. Major irreversible changes are signaled through understandable world feedback. Essential progression has fallback routes so creative rearrangement does not silently strand the story.
+
+## Fictional NPCs and side quests
+
+Every interactable NPC has contextual information, a hint or a side-quest opportunity appropriate to current state. They can remember completed help, react to construction and introduce others. They need not recite a quest every time. An unavailable or completed task produces an honest alternate response.
+
+| Proposed NPC | Place/personality | Hint | Side quest and branches |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| The Caretaker | Quiet maintenance inhabitant near the vestibule | Notices a circuit still responding | A Light for the Table: power a lamp by repair, rerouting or a newly built source; the chosen method changes the later maintenance task |
+| The Archivist | Patient organizer in the Pencil Room | Explains that a missing index does not mean a page is gone | A Place for This Page: recover or rebuild catalog access, then choose an arrangement; recognition respects private/shared display choices |
+| The Courier | Restless route-finder at the service threshold | Knows an alternate supply entrance | The Long Way Home: repair a lift, build a bridge or open a tunnel; NPC travel and future deliveries follow the actual route |
+| The Tinkerer | Curious builder in the machine bay | Spots spare capacity or an incompatible part | Something Worth Sharing: assemble a communal machine or adapt an existing one; its purpose shapes later visitors and resource needs |
+| The Listener | Unhurried visitor near the garden | Reflects on an observed choice without claiming knowledge of private memories | Leave a Place to Sit: create an accessible place to pause; later conversations vary with the resulting space |
+
+The five supportive NPC roles above are fictional proposals. Antagonists draw from the negative figures in Echos Within under the creator's direction; each requires a source-based character profile before assigning encounters or quest roles. Additional NPCs follow the same hint/task/relationship contract. Adam remains the remembered brother guiding BS across the whole place.
+
+## Adam's guidance and memory
+
+Adam recalls actual actions and relates them to current possibilities. New example game dialogue, not historical quotations:
+
+- At the desk: "We can keep something here. Start with what you want to remember."
+- After a player-built shortcut: "You made a way back."
+- When a plan changes: "We can use what you learned from that."
+
+Personal characterization follows the creator's memories of familiarity, humor, encouragement and safety. Comfortable silence and shared-play moments remain available. Adam's interpretation distinguishes remembered biography, fictional lore and the current branch's actions.
+
+Keep separate records for world state, action history, NPC knowledge, relationships, quest stages, chosen source memories, and cue delivery. Preserve causal IDs so a new unlock can explain what caused it. Undo, demolition and checkpoint rollback re-evaluate dependent conditions without rewriting biographical facts.
+
+## Outcomes and chapter departures
+
+The hub can become an archive-led refuge, a maker's workshop, a connected meeting place or a mixture. These are descriptive outcomes based on actual builds and choices, not personality judgments or one hidden morality score.
+
+Available departures can include Archives first, Core Sinks first, a construction-led network route, or an NPC-led side expedition. Later returns change room use, visitors and Adam's contextual recall. Paths can reconverge while preserving their different histories.
+
+Longer-term endings use several dimensions: what was preserved, built, connected, shared and left unfinished, along with NPC/quest outcomes and the final player choice. Selective authored ending scenes can combine those facts. No ending changes the real brother's life or treats grief as a task completed by winning.
+
+## Next planning deliverables
+
+1. A room-by-room diagram with editable doors, machine sockets, seats, route zones and NPC anchors.
+2. A selected personal-memory list with origin, intended meaning and proposed presentation.
+3. A first-hour branch graph showing at least three ways through the hub and their later consequences.
+4. A rule/NPC/quest authoring contract with previews explaining affected dependencies and save compatibility.
+5. A consistency walkthrough: open/close, build/dismantle, depart/return, help/decline and save/reload each route; verify coherent hints and alternate progression.
+
+All of this is narrative/system planning. No engine implementation, generated voice, SDK installation or source-manuscript edit is included.
